<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pagan Min</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1811–1880</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pagan Min was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> from 1846 to 1853, the son of Tharrawaddy. His reign was marked by the execution of rivals and arbitrary rule, and a dispute over the treatment of British ship captains at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Rangoon</a> led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-burmese-war">Second Anglo-Burmese War</a> in 1852, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a>. He was deposed by his half-brother Mindon in 1853 and lived on in retirement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pagan-min.jpg" alt="Pagan Min" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Archaeological Survey of India · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_Bagan_Min%27s_mother.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
